-- title:
--   Mirror identity for the vertical-line test factor: $H_k(1-c-it) = H_{k(-\cdot)}(c+it)$
-- statement:
--   For a test function $k : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{C}$, the vertical-line test factor is $H_k(s) := h_k\big((s - 1/2)/i\big)$, where $h_k(z) = \int_{\mathbb{R}} k(u)e^{izu}\,du$ is the paper Fourier transform; $H_k$ is the function integrated against $\zeta'/\zeta$ along vertical lines in the contour proof of the explicit formula.
--
--   The theorem asserts, for all real $c$ and $t$,
--   $$H_k(1 - c - it) \;=\; H_{k(-\cdot)}(c + it),$$
--   where $k(-\cdot)$ denotes the reflected test function $u \mapsto k(-u)$. That is, evaluating the test factor on the left vertical line $\operatorname{Re} s = 1-c$ (traversed with the mirrored ordinate) is the same as evaluating the test factor of the reflected test function on the right line $\operatorname{Re} s = c$.
--
--   In the project this identity is used in the assembly of the explicit formula, `Zeta23.WeilEF.EF_lit_zeta` (its sole consumer): via the functional equation, the contribution of the left edge of the contour is converted into a right-edge integral for $k(-\cdot)$, which is how the prime sum acquires both terms $k(\log n)$ and $k(-\log n)$.
